Access Control (in Java) means controlling who can access classes, methods, variables, and constructors.
This is done using access modifiers.

Why Access Control is needed

Protect data from unauthorized access
Increase security
Support encapsulation
Control visibility of class members

Access Modifiers in Java
Modifier	Same Class	Same Package	Subclass (other pkg)	Other Classes
private     	✔	        ✘	               ✘	                 ✘
default 	    ✔	        ✔	               ✘	                 ✘
protected	    ✔	        ✔	               ✔	                 ✘
public	        ✔	        ✔	               ✔	                 ✔


1. private
Accessible only inside the same class
Used to hide data

class Test {
    private int x = 10;

    private void show() {
        System.out.println(x);
    }
}

2. default (package-private)
No keyword used
Accessible within same package only

class Sample {
    int a = 5;   // default

    void display() {
        System.out.println(a);
    }
}

3. protected
Accessible in same package
Accessible in subclasses outside package

class Parent {
    protected int data = 100;
}

4. public
Accessible from anywhere

public class Demo {
    public int value = 20;

    public void show() {
        System.out.println(value);
    }
}

Real-life Example

Think of a bank account:
private → ATM PIN
default → Bank staff
protected → Branch managers
public → Bank name